# The `system` producer: the `mos-system` package -- the common system policy,
# state mounts, seed and reconcile tools, units and operator account that every
# mos image carries whatever board it is for.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no command substitution, no
# expansion. Safe to source from bash and to parse with grep/sed from anything
# else, and checked for that shape by build-env/deb/build.sh before it is
# sourced. build-env/deb/README.md documents every field.

PACKAGES="mos-system"

# `all`. The payload is shell scripts, unit files and configuration -- there is
# no ELF anywhere in it -- so one archive is correct for every image this
# repository builds, and the board-dependent files (fstab, fw_env.config, the
# uenv repart definitions, boot.mount) are deliberately NOT here: they are
# rendered from board.env and belong to the board packages.
ARCHES="all"

# `family` is rootfs/packages-src itself, and it is here for one file: the
# shared copyright. The primary build context is this producer's OWN
# directory, so anything held in common with the sibling producers sits one
# level up and has to be named to be reachable.
#
# The payload's source tree, handed over as a named context rather than copied
# into this directory. rootfs/overlay is what stages/20-install installs
# today and stays the single source of those bytes; a copy beside this producer
# would be a second one that drifts.
BUILD_CONTEXTS="family=rootfs/packages-src overlay=rootfs/overlay"

# The five units this package enables at multi-user.target: mos-shadow-reconcile,
# mos-health, mos-machine-id, systemd-networkd and systemd-resolved.
#
# It ships seventeen more enablement links than those five, and this field
# counts NONE of them: eleven under local-fs.target.wants for the state mounts,
# one under timers.target.wants for fstrim, four that enabling
# systemd-networkd.service means beyond its primary link -- its `Alias=`
# dbus-org.freedesktop.network1.service and the `Also=` links under
# sockets.target.wants, network-online.target.wants and sysinit.target.wants --
# and openssh's `Alias=` /etc/systemd/system/sshd.service, which this package
# owns because the preset it also ships stops deb-systemd-helper writing it.
#
# It counts the multi-user set alone, because that is the one question every
# producer here can be asked identically; the rest of the links are ordinary
# payload, and the byte-for-byte checks are what speak about them. Enabling a
# unit whose [Install] carries `Alias=` or `Also=` therefore moves the payload
# and leaves this number where it is -- five is a count of DECISIONS, not of
# symlinks. The DISABLE decision this package also makes is not a link at all:
# /usr/lib/systemd/system-preset/50-mos-ssh.preset keeps ssh.service off, and no
# count of symlinks anywhere can see it.
ENABLEMENT="mos-system=5"
